<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick II of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1559. In his first year he bought the bishoprics of Ösel-Wiek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> for his younger brother Magnus of Holstein, drawing Denmark into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war">Northern Seven Years' War</a> of 1563–1570, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a> castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a>, and gave the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ven-island">Hven</a> for his observatory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Frederick II of Denmark" width="320" height="638" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Knieper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1581_Frederik_2..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 1 July 1534 at Haderslevhus Castle, the son of Duke Christian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein, later King Christian III, and Dorothea of Saxe-Lauenburg. His father's victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a> had restored royal power, and Christian III's coronation charter made the king's eldest son heir apparent. Frederick's education was thorough but centred on theology, and a wider princely programme planned by the Danish Chancellor was never carried out. He learned to write clearly but struggled with reading and spelling.</p><p>In 1554 he was allowed his own court at Malmö Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, under a court master, and began political training there. His closest political tutor was his brother-in-law, Elector Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Christian III died on 1 January 1559, and within weeks Frederick joined his uncles in Holstein in a campaign under Johan Rantzau that conquered Ditmarschen, which King John had failed to subdue in 1500. He had not consulted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a>, and the councillor Johan Friis had warned of a threat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In 1560 he installed his brother Magnus of Holstein as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ösel-Wiek, and in 1562 he made peace with Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a>. The competition with Sweden broke into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-seven-years-war">Northern Seven Years' War</a> in 1563.</p><p>In peacetime Frederick moved his court from residence to residence, hunting and feasting with councillors, and he did most of his business with the Council one to one. The crown's revenues rose after the war, paying for a larger fleet, the first national road network, the kongevej, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Elsinore</a>. In 1583 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> agreed to pay annually for permission to sail in what he called the king's sound. He issued a Marriage Ordinance in 1582 that allowed divorce on grounds including infidelity and leprosy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1572 Frederick married his cousin Sophie of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, and they had seven children. He was a patron of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, where he raised the budget, supported poor students and funded study abroad. He backed Paracelsian medicine and, through his interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a> and astrology, the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>, who received the island of Ven and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> to fund his work at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a>.</p><p>He died on 4 April 1588 at Antvorskov, aged 53, and was buried at Roskilde Cathedral. Danish historians long portrayed him as unlettered and brutish, a view begun by Troels Frederik Lund in 1906 and continued by Erik Arup. Historians such as Poul Grinder-Hansen and Paul Douglas Lockhart now regard his reign, especially the later years, as a success.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick II of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tycho Brahe (1546–1601) was a Danish nobleman who built Uraniborg with the support of <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b> and died as imperial mathematician to Rudolf II in Prague.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Work began on Tycho Brahe's observatory on the island of Hven, granted to him by <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1559 Bishop Johann von Münchhausen sold the bishopric to <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b>, who gave it to his brother Magnus of Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing the Livonian War, Bishop Johann von Münchhausen sold the bishopric to <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b> in 1559, and the king passed it to his brother Magnus of Holstein, who arrived in 1560.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-osel-wiek">Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1559 Bishop Johann von Münchhausen sold the Bishopric of Ösel-Wiek to <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b>, who gave it to his brother Magnus of Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-osel">Danish Ösel</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan IV of Russia, seeking to win the Baltic Germans to his side in the Livonian War, proclaimed Magnus of Holstein, brother of <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b> and ruler of Danish Ösel, king of Livonia in 1570.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-livonia">Kingdom of Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haderslev, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Slagelse, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.25,9.5&amp;year=1569" aria-label="Haderslev on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4758 277 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick II of Denmark"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 547h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 547h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.25,9.5&amp;year=1569">What was here in 1569? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
